("modern", "Here's the short version: your sourdough isn't rising because the starter is under-fed, not because your flour is wrong. A starter that doubles in four to six hours is ready to bake with. If yours takes twelve, it's alive but sluggish, and that sluggishness carries straight into the dough. The fix is simple. Feed it twice a day at a 1:1:1 ratio for three or four days, keep it somewhere around 24 to 26 degrees, and use it at its peak, when it's domed and just starting to fall. You'll also want to stretch your bulk ferment. Most recipes assume a warm kitchen. In a cooler one, bulk can take eight hours rather than four, and cutting it short is the most common reason loaves come out dense. Watch the dough, not the clock."),
("modern", "This matters because most people misunderstand what a credit score actually measures. It isn't a judgement of your wealth or your income. It's a prediction of how likely you are to repay borrowed money on time, based almost entirely on your past behaviour with credit. That's why someone earning a modest salary can have an excellent score while a high earner with a short or messy history might not. Payment history carries the most weight, followed by how much of your available credit you're using. The practical takeaway is straightforward. Pay every bill on time, keep your balances well below your limits, and avoid opening several new accounts at once. Do those three things consistently and your score will take care of itself."),
("modern", "The Treaty of Versailles didn't just end the First World War — it set the conditions for the next one. Germany lost territory, its colonies, and most of its military, and it was forced to accept responsibility for the war and pay heavy reparations. Those terms landed on a country already reeling from defeat and economic collapse. The result wasn't peace so much as resentment. Hyperinflation in the early 1920s wiped out savings, and the Depression a decade later hit Germany especially hard. Extremist parties exploited that anger, and the Nazis in particular framed the treaty as a national humiliation that only they could undo. Historians still debate how much blame the treaty deserves, but few would argue it helped. It's a reminder that how a war ends can matter as much as how it's fought."),
("modern", "Short answer: yes, you can run MagicMirror on a Pi 3B+, but you'll want to trim it down. The 3B+ has 1GB of RAM, and the default setup runs Electron, which is essentially a full Chromium browser. That works, but it's tight once you add more than a handful of modules. A few things help. Use a lightweight OS image without the desktop. Stick to modules that update every few minutes rather than every few seconds. And avoid anything that streams video or renders animated backgrounds. If you still see lag, you can run MagicMirror in server-only mode and point a browser at it, which takes some load off. None of this is difficult. It just means being a bit choosy about what goes on the screen."),
("modern", "Good question, and it's one people often get backwards. Antibiotics don't work on viruses at all. They target structures and processes that bacteria have, such as cell walls, and that viruses simply don't. So taking antibiotics for a cold or the flu won't shorten it or ease the symptoms. It can actually cause harm. Every unnecessary course gives bacteria in your body another chance to develop resistance, and resistant infections are much harder to treat. That's why doctors are increasingly reluctant to prescribe them for coughs and sore throats. If you do have a bacterial infection, such as strep throat, antibiotics are genuinely useful. The key is knowing which is which, and that's a call your doctor can make with a quick exam or test."),
("modern", "Moving to a four-day work week sounds radical, but the evidence so far is surprisingly consistent. In the large UK pilot run in 2022, most participating companies kept the arrangement after the trial ended, and many reported steady or improved productivity alongside lower burnout. The catch is that it isn't simply the same work squeezed into fewer days. The firms that succeeded cut low-value meetings, tightened their processes, and were clear about what really needed doing. Those that didn't plan tended to see staff working longer hours on the remaining days, which defeats the point. So the real lesson isn't about the number of days. It's about whether an organisation is willing to look hard at how its time is spent. That's the part most companies skip."),
("modern", "Black holes aren't cosmic vacuum cleaners. That's the first thing worth clearing up. From far away, a black hole's gravity behaves just like that of any other object with the same mass. If the Sun were replaced by a black hole of equal mass tomorrow, Earth would keep orbiting exactly as it does now. It would just get very cold and dark. What makes black holes strange is what happens up close. Inside a boundary called the event horizon, the escape velocity exceeds the speed of light, so nothing, not even light, can get back out. That's why we can't see them directly. Instead, astronomers detect them by their effects: stars whipping around an unseen companion, or gas heating up and glowing as it spirals inward."),
("modern", "Let's break this down. The question asks you to prove that the square root of 2 is irrational, and the standard approach is proof by contradiction. You start by assuming the opposite: that root 2 can be written as a fraction a over b in lowest terms. Squaring both sides gives 2 equals a squared over b squared, so a squared equals 2b squared. That means a squared is even, which forces a to be even too. Write a as 2k. Substituting back gives 4k squared equals 2b squared, so b squared equals 2k squared, and b must be even as well. But now a and b are both even, which contradicts the assumption that the fraction was in lowest terms. So the assumption must be false, and root 2 is irrational."),
("modern", "Honestly, the best study method is the one you'll actually stick with, but a few techniques have strong evidence behind them. Active recall is the big one. Instead of rereading your notes, close the book and try to write down everything you remember, then check what you missed. It feels harder, and that difficulty is exactly why it works. Spaced repetition is the second. Reviewing material a day later, then a few days later, then a week later beats cramming it all in one night. Third, mix up your practice. Doing a set of problems from different topics forces you to figure out which method each one needs, which is what an exam will ask of you. Pick one of these and try it this week. You'll notice the difference."),
